 of the function $f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}-y-{z}^{2}$ on the surface ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186124824 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-1,-2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x=-2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y \\\\ -2z=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)x=0 \\\\ 2\\lambda y=-1 \\\\ z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Using the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 1,0).$\n\n- Now, for the value $\\lambda =0$ we obtain a contradiction in the above system.\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,-1,0),$ and its maximum value is $1.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123236 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186125111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366026 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125111 1071 "Find a basis of $Ker(T)$ given that $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-9x - 3y \\\\ -12x - 4y\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186125111 1071 "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y \\\\ -12x-4y\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125111 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{3}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123392 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186125137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366078 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125137 1071 "Consider the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of $R^{2}$ given by\n$C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$.\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the map $T: V → R^{2}$ relative to these bases if\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{3}) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186125137 1071 "Consider the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125137 1074 "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 21\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123464 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186125149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366097 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125149 1071 "The basis $B$ of $R^{3}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & b & -5 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & c\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the value of $a + b + c$, given that\n\n$T(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}x + y + z \\\\ x - y \\\\ x - z\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186125149 1071 "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & b & -5 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ x-y \\\\ x-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186125149 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -3 & -1 & -10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $b=-2,$ $c=-2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+(-2)+(-2)=-1.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186129138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366287 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] 