function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R$, and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186129861 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [-6,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186129861 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =3(-2-(-5))(-1-(-6)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=45\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 45=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=45.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186129881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128737 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186129881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366399 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186129881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186129881 1071 "The domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x + 3}{3x - 2a}$ is $(- ∞, 2)∪(2, ∞)$. What is the value of $a$?" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186129881 1071 "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{3x-2a}$ is $(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ What is the value of $a?$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186129881 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2a & =0 \\\\ 3x & =2a \\\\ x & =\\frac{2a}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{2a}{3}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=2$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{2a}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{a}{3} & =1 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366117 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365009 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365177 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366131 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366385 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366531 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133801 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366124 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133801 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366392 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133803 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366138 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133803 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366703 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366145 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366510 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365072 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366503 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365044 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365051 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133955 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365030 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133956 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365037 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133956 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366482 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366489 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186133957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366496 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186147431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366218 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186147433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366225 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366273 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366177 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366538 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366545 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152179 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366552 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366573 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366190 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366580 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364729 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364736 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366614 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365390 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365397 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150029 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186152592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366006 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6$?\n\n1. $R_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$\n2. $R^{5}$\n3. $M_{6}(R)$, the vector space of all $6 \\cdot 6$ matrices over $R$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186152592 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6?$\n\n- ${R}_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$\n- ${R}^{5}$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{6}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 6$ matrices over $R$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152592 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{5}[t])=5+1=6.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{5})=5\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6}(R))={6}^{2}=36\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150227 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186152625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366184 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-9t{y}^{′}+41y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186152625 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 9ty^{′} + 41y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 1, t > 0$." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186152625 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-9t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+41{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+41{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +41] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25)-25+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -5 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{5+4\\text{i}} & ={t}^{5}{e}^{\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{5-4\\text{i}} & ={t}^{5}{e}^{-\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{5+4\\text{i}}+B{t}^{5-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{5}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=1$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={t}^{4}((5{c}_{2}-4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+(4{c}_{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4{c}_{2}+5 \\\\ -4 & =4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186161811 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\n- $A$ is diagonalizable\n\n- If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186161811 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n2. $A$ is diagonalizable\n3. If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186161811 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186161811 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186161812 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix ![](images/55/554407e7ec109f5d6e767d87900321d07c253cceefd6e1cdd21ec11351c4548e.png)" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186161812 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186161812 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively.\n\nMoreover, $\\mathbf u_i\\cdot \\mathbf u_i^T$ is the projection matrix corresponding to $\\mathbf u_i$.\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=-1$. Computing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac12x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=2$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nDividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot u^{T} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186161812 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, $\\mathbf u_i\\cdot \\mathbf u_i^T$ is the projection matrix corresponding to $\\mathbf u_i.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac12x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/3e/3e365c1786b35c426487c5555bccdb10c67545e5757c8e09b34c514d41ce9219.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/e9/e97e23e38548021d0a72c50ff7e34ed108070c482249b23f9cb54a6e812ecc77.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n![](images/23/23384122e6