^{2}m/s^{2}$, where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O$. If $P$ has velocity $v = 16m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $20m$, find the value of $k$." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186179920 1074 "![](images/4c/4c997edf5c34a22fc71eac697c161434797c938df40760ae1089fd6e62db795d.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $k{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-k{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{3}{x}^{3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=16\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(16)^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{3}(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=128-\\frac{k}{3}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=20\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =128-\\frac{k}{3}{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =128-\\frac{k}{3}(20)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{8,000k}{3} & =128 \\\\ 8,000k & =384 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177699 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186180010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364920 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180010 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-2$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180010 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = 3u - v + 2, y = v - u$.\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) =-2$\n3. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186180010 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178251 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186180102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365246 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180102 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x \\ge 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, and the acceleration is $(3 - x)m/s^{2}$. If $P$ has velocity $v = 2m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186180102 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(3-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180102 1074 "![](images/0f/0f8f7773a5e55c81de553eccf684f9a6128dd6e76e06b9040d5953371e72184f.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=3\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $1>0$ | $0$ | $-1<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=3\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (3-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =3(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =6x-{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(3)=\\sqrt{6(3)-(3)^{2}+4}=\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178263 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186180104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365329 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180104 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=6\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $9\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186180104 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt{x}m/s^{2}$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v = 6m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v = 6\\sqrt{6}m/s$ when its displacement is $9m$, find the value of $k$." 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186180104 1074 "![](images/b0/b052a65fb0dbcc14a5d359b693beafdf5da3f4bbe1e10f006c315e8168340d5c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\sqrt{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (k\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}(0)^{3/2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+18.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=6\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=9\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}(9)^{3/2}+18 \\\\ 108 & =18k+18 \\\\ 18k & =90 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186188210 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$. If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1$, then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$?" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186188210 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3.$ If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1,$ then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186188210 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(T) + nullity(T) &= n \\\\\nrank(T) + 1 &= 3 \\\\\nrank(T) &= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186188210 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+1 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186188213 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and let $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n2. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n3. $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186188213 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 