^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(4) & =2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3\\cdot 4}=2{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186217809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216684 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186217809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365901 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186217809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186217809 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x}{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=6.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186217809 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} + \\frac{2}{x}y^{′} + y = 0, x > 0$.\nSuppose $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1) = 6$. What is $W(3)$?$W(3) =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186217809 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}} \\\\ 6 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365737 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365665 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365674 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365679 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221429 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365639 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365646 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365651 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186221431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365656 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186228930 1071 "Consider a spherical  planet with radius $(6.3\\times10^6)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 9.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$ Which differential equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet, where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object? Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186228930 1071 "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(6.3\\times10^6)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 9.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Which differential equation best models the 