orname{sin}⁡x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186229717 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} + y = \\sec^{3} x$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n$\\cos 2x = 1 - 2\\sin^{2} x$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186229717 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={sec}^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231297 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'-6y = f(x), \\qquad y(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p(1)=0.$*\n\n![](images/d9/d95dc53c303ddf919518e2270cfe6f4314712b414a65f54fb7d228783fa0e214.png)" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231297 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n$y'-6y = f(x), \\qquad y(1)=4$\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p(1)=0$.*\n\n$AAA?AAA$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186231297 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of $y' - 6y = 0$ is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator $y' - 6y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim Ae^{6x} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=4$. Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(1) &= Ae^{6(1)} + y_{p}(1) \\\\\n4 &= Ae^{6} + 0 \\\\\nA &= 4e^{-6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\ny_c = (4e^{-6})e^{6x} = 4e^{6x-6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim \\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{4e^{6x-6}} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186231297 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form $y' + P(x)y = f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of $y' - 6y = 0$ is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator $y' - 6y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim Ae^{6x} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=4.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{6(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{6}+0 \\\\ A & =4{e}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\ny_c = (4e^{-6})e^{6x} = 4e^{6x-6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) \\sim \n\\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{4e^{6x-6}} +\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (a_n \\cos(n\\pi x) + b_n \\sin(n\\pi x)).\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231299 1071 "$$\ny''+ 7\\pi^2 y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n![](images/9f/9f77e2dc9619cf5cb270cd68efaf2432a8dbf738e06b33dcdcc18602b5032b1b.png)" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231299 1071 "$y''+ 7\\pi^2 y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0$\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$AAA?AAA$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186231299 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution $y_p$. Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 7\\pi^2 = 0\n$$\n\ngives roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pm \\sqrt{7}\\pi \\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= A\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) + y_{p}(0) \\\\\n0 &= A(1) + 0 + 0 \\\\\nA &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $y_p(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition $y'(0)=0$. First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\ny'(x) \\sim B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi)\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n\\pi^2\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying $y'(0)=0$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= B(\\sqrt{7}π) + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\nB(\\sqrt{7}π) &= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\nB &= -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}π}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(28 - n^{2})) \\\\\n&= - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(8(-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{7}nπ^{3}(28 - n^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into\n\n$$\ny_c = B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\n$$\n\nwe find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{-\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{\\sqrt{7}n\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)}} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 false] [17592186231299 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form $y'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)$ is given by $y = y_c + y_p,$ where $y_c$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution $y_p.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation $\\lambda^2 + 7\\pi^2 = 0$ gives roots $\\lambda = \\pm \\sqrt{7}\\pi \\textrm i.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $y_p(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition $y'(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\ny'(x) \\sim B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi)\\cos(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n\\pi^2\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying $y'(0)=0$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(28-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B(\\sqrt{7}\\pi ) & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(28-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(28-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{7}n{\\pi }^{3}(28-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into $y_c = B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \n\\bbox[4pt,border: 0.5px solid black]{-\\sin(\\sqrt{7}\\pi x)\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{\\sqrt{7}n\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)}}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\dfrac{16(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^3\\left(28-n^2\\right)} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365939 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365942 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365945 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365948 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365951 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365933 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365954 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365960 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186231425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365966 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364662 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364663 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364664 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364665 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364666 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364663 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364661 1080 #uuid "2d034eaf-f3c1-5445-a2e7-a4d722a419ee" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364662 1081 "4{z}^{2}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364662 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] 