7592186364668 1084 #uuid "66f7a2b8-23c9-5110-8e24-f664a2447594" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364669 1081 "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364669 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364669 1084 #uuid "8b83da41-3c56-5bc4-85a9-468d168ca70b" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364670 1081 "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364670 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364670 1084 #uuid "775f11c6-37a0-5ac7-b461-a5d7115f3767" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364671 1081 "3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364671 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364671 1084 #uuid "cc1b2546-f11f-5044-a149-48fa7c0b8940" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364672 1081 "3{t}^{2}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364672 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364672 1084 #uuid "0ff668a4-f940-589a-8790-3b71b79abc55" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364674 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364673 1069 "q-104173" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364673 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$x+y-\\sqrt{z}=0$\nfor $z\\ge 0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364673 1072 #uuid "f7fc0db6-4abf-5f9e-a0cc-186a2d153719" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364673 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-\\sqrt{z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{z} & =x+y \\\\ z & =(x+y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,(x+y)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,(u+v)^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $(u+v)^{2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that $u+v\\ge 0.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364675 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364676 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364677 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364678 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364679 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364679 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364674 1080 #uuid "50859c1e-f37e-52ce-84ca-b439c660573d" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364675 1081 "{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364675 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364675 1084 #uuid "6831c1de-2d22-5f83-a124-3bff0cc917c7" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364676 1081 "\\sqrt{u+v}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364676 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364676 1084 #uuid "593a18ab-3f63-54c6-b541-e09ad6470169" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364677 1081 "\\sqrt{u}+\\sqrt{v}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364677 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364677 1084 #uuid "c9cc9fac-739c-5246-a226-7d78e70210ff" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364678 1081 "u+v" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364678 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364678 1084 #uuid "0f95f694-3fb7-5f4b-8a37-e75be6da3d8d" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364679 1081 "(u+v)^{2}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364679 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364679 1084 #uuid "500641ec-9b98-524a-b832-ab57fd32c7bd" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364681 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364680 1069 "q-104224" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364680 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $r=6$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364680 1072 #uuid "f5f2e716-9b66-530a-bc7e-f09e3da3b617" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364680 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=6\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $6,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66a12bea76428d4cdfff54210546dfea8b0124c1dff1b4986d7bb6b814f34a2f.png)" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364682 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364683 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364684 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364685 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364686 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364686 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364681 1080 #uuid "853597e9-85d5-5c98-99a7-5367188bd89b" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364682 1081 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364682 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364682 1084 #uuid "71c382ac-1eac-5abc-95df-6def14541172" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364683 1081 "A sphere" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364683 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364683 1084 #uuid "6e74e9a3-6670-5588-9023-b750f1f372ed" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364684 1081 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364684 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364684 1084 #uuid "eaddfd2d-93c8-5fee-8bad-9d3d4cccb9d5" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364685 1081 "A half-cone" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364685 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364685 1084 #uuid "7a2bde51-684c-5e48-b2a6-b08aed5f08e9" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364686 1081 "A circular cylinder" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364686 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364686 1084 #uuid "c424832f-b884-5818-8b53-81947b568c74" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364688 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364687 1069 "q-104980" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364687 1071 "The function $f(x,y)$ has Hessian matrix\n\n$$\n{∇}^{2}f(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2(y-3) & 2(x+1) \\\\ 2(x+1) & (y-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(-1,3)<0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,3)>0$\n- The second partial derivative test is inconclusive" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364687 1072 #uuid "a2adfa73-7e18-5d5d-8163-694e5efa8a14" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364687 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ then:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$;\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a.$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a.$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivative test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the Hessian at $(-1,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∇}^{2}f(-1,3)=[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+2(3-3) & 2(-1+1) \\\\ 2(-1+1) & (3-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The Hessian determinant is\n\n$$\nH(-1,3)=det({∇}^{2}f(-1,3))=1(0)-0(0)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true because\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,3)=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $H(-1,3)=0,$ so the second partial derivative test is inconclusive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364688 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364689 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364688 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364690 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364688 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364691 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364688 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364692 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364688 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364693 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364688 1076 #edb/ref 17592186