2186364694 1072 #uuid "265e39ea-f8c9-5146-9ac4-7988205838c0" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364694 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & 6y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4=0 \\\\ 6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-4)=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,0)=det({f}^{″}(2,0))=2\\cdot 6-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,0)=({f}^{″}(2,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0).$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364696 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364697 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364698 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364699 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364700 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364698 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364695 1080 #uuid "a892e43b-67a8-5cd6-aed3-b41a3dd2b871" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364696 1084 #uuid "e870df35-a29a-5740-b1a9-fc27a59659f0" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,0)$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186364697 1084 #uuid "3dc15fc6-ed64-52