ext{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4cd2d122764f82a6c38a9d657dcc579d2b93e676266604e03188ac933710dab0.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}-1\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/69/69b31f925d432360dc0796c3745782bacb6645bbabcf159f9049770c09359757.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by \n\n$$\nx={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1=2.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dafed2ddc7ae18a05ec4fe1d5768f9b0f12456e0d2cf2552e91382d686d65522.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}-1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365206 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365207 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365206 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365208 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365206 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365209 13194139533447 175921860