(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{11})^{2} & =k(6(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+2 \\\\ 22 & =10k+2 \\\\ 10k & =20 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =12x-{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =24x-2{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+24x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+24x+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(6)=\\sqrt{-2(6)^{2}+24(6)+4}=2\\sqrt{19}\\,m/s\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365324 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365325 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365326 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365327 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365328 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365326 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365323 1080 #uuid "4aa0d68b-3aee-5717-aebf-0211366a9b34" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365324 1081 "8\\,m/s" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365324 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365324 1084 #uuid "dcef8185-08c1-5b71-bc52-921851bf0b0a" 13194139533447 17592186045585 tr