-9179-33b1a5a738c5" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365620 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365620 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365620 1084 #uuid "1500ac59-78fb-5766-95a3-7fe669acd14f" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365621 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365621 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365621 1084 #uuid "00662630-714a-50e6-8e55-0bb5957ebe04" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365622 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365622 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365622 1084 #uuid "a8425d9e-706f-546f-880e-68197d0b9555" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365624 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365623 1069 "q-332538" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365623 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-4x-5.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365623 1072 #uuid "55f882dd-0d2d-545e-b892-57f2a195b2ee" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365623 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x+(\\alpha +4\\beta )=-4x-5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-x-1.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365625 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365624 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365624 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365624 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365625 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365624 1080 #uuid "7e0b9a7c-af99-5ec5-90de-2d525ca3c011" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365625 1081 "A{e}^{-4x}-x-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365625 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365625 1084 #uuid "9dc63f4c-9e83-58cc-8d45-61ba1787bc8f" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365627 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365626 1069 "q-332702" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365626 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365626 1072 #uuid "4ee20fe7-9c20-5484-879f-710a92d2abcd" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365626 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365627 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365628 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365627 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365629 131941