8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(\\alpha -6\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6(0)}-2(0)-\\frac{5}{3} & =2 \\\\ A-\\frac{5}{3} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{3}\\,{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365634 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365635 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365634 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365634 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365634 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365635 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365634 1080 #uuid "9c5af851-8b99-5ee8-ab29-3944df50aa73" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365635 1081 "\\frac{11}{3}{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365635 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365635 1084 #uuid "b6311dbc-76a3-5ea5-8492-1ac8994a78ce" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365637 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365636 1069 "q-333058" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365636 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y=3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y=3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365636 1072 #uuid "3b4fb91c-5bf2-54eb-af95-268e2d101d2f" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365636 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\beta =3\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}+3 & =-1 \\\\ A+3 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,{e}^{-x}+3.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365637 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365638 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365637 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365637 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365637 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365638 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365637 1080 #uuid "87c875b6-0ddd-59aa-8855-787f75ca3e38" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365638 1081 "-4{e}^{-x}+3" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365638 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365638 1084 #uuid "12a11ec5-9e9e-548f-a094-e36cfc2aa8c8" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365640 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365642 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365644 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1069 "q-333399" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-2y={x}^{2},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1072 #uuid "5aa9ffd7-8e70-5486-8c98-c4f81e1514d2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365639 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-2y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-4x{y}^{′}+2y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=-4xz+2y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(0)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-4xz+2y+{x}^{2},\\, & z(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and $z(0)=-1,$ we place $y=2$ and $z=-1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-4xz+2y+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+(-1)=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+4=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈1.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365640 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365641 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365640 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365640 1077 "field-3" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365640 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365641 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365640 1080 #uuid "0140e720-f76b-520b-aa24-31f9d388b7ec" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365641 1081 "1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365641 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365641 1084 #uuid "87c6a003-fe91-5a09-a621-17ee211874e3" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365643 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365642 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365642 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365642 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365643 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365642 1080 #uuid "7dcb8e82-9094-5e4b-8cf2-9b57be7acd86" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365643 1081 "-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365643 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365643 1084 #uuid "abecd009-0855-5118-a859-998bbc8be6cb" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365644 1075 #edb/ref 175