nstant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=(7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m},$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =-\\frac{k}{(7\\times {10}^{7})^{2}} \\\\ k & =8\\cdot (7\\times {10}^{7})^{2} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (7)^{2}\\times ({10}^{7})^{2} \\\\  & =392\\times {10}^{14} \\\\  & =3.92\\times {10}^{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{3.92\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{3.92\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365690 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365691 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365692 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365693 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365694 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365692 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365689 1080 #uuid "06b98b0a-fe81-57d2-a0fc-2e38e5b1fad7" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365690 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{3.80\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 