ject. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r\\to \\infty ,$ the expression $\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}$ approaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\n{v}_{e}=\\sqrt{2gR}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values $R=(1.7\\times {10}^{7})$ and $g=3.3,$ we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{e} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7})\\cdot 3.3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 1.7\\cdot 3.3)\\times {10}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11.22\\times {10}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.122\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.122}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & ≈(1.06\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365783 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365784 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365783 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365783 1077 "field-2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365783 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365784 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365783 1080 #uuid "61d0a1e5-b37a-5477-a708-5cf3ca01de76" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365784 1081 "{10}^{4}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365784 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365784 1084 #uuid "d7d37d45-6702-