ng a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the train has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the train moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<t<$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365972 1072 #uuid "6916e525-178d-5e14-99f4-1c9df87974e0" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365972 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(5)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(5)^{2}-5(5)+C \\\\ 0 & =25-25+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the train at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the train is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t=t(t-5)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/af/afe458cdaf5eedbfb78cd9067b0c8caa209535391156bb5de14de34562f3cc1c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever  $0\\,<\\,t<5.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365974 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365973 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365973 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365973 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365974 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365973 1080 #uuid "b67b2d9b-211f-5c47-bad5-b506e027b04c" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365974 1081 "0" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365974 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365974 1084 #uuid "b61a3efe-9fc2-5947-8f09-21fd105ac642" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365976 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365975 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365975 1077 "field-2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365975 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365976 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365975 1080 #uuid "951ff62b-dd1d-5161-94a1-d23ccfa6e632" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365976 1081 "5" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365976 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365976 1084 #uuid "fa68554c-8bc2-59ac-8c0d-32c14b4bd2ce" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365978 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365980 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1069 "q-351811" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1071 "A skateboarder moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-6,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the skateboarder has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the skateboarder moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1072 #uuid "1a25e42d-678a-5750-8cff-a81689be643d" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365977 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-6)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(1)^{3}-3(1)^{2}-6(1)+C \\\\ 0 & =1-3-6+C \\\\ C & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the skateboarder at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-6t+8.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the skateboarder is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-6t+8=(t-1)({t}^{2}-2t-8)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/c8/c86cdd70316b0bdb620a9a3224823be70069328e0aac83d2c0add2b925323423.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365979 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365978 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365978 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365978 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365979 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365978 1080 #uuid "4d7f2738-e98b-54aa-b2eb-8252980bd9ed" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365979 1081 "1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365979 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365979 1084 #uuid "7efbe3ba-518d-501d-8539-f183c63ae5d7" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365980 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365981 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365980 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365980 1077 "field-2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365980 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365981 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365980 1080 #uuid "c605d975-c6f4-5fc8-a749-a990b683fadc" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365981 1081 "4" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365981 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365981 1084 #uuid "d06df830-42fd-5904-9966-2a1ce30c5f03" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365983 13194139533447 1