"ad675689-ca09-5d45-915a-9e784e5baf6f" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365984 1081 "3" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365984 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365984 1084 #uuid "0d3d80ea-bb47-5864-bdb6-13dbd6ebfbb4" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365986 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365988 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1069 "q-354844" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1071 "A runner moves along a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-10,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the runner is stationary when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the runner moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1072 #uuid "f5317749-27b3-5359-9496-b38cef818abb" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365985 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-10)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-10t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(5)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}-10(5)+C \\\\ 0 & =125-75-50+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the runner at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-10t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the runner is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-10t=t(t-5)(t+2)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/0e/0e6c4aca5cba41c90fb369f0b6d820f15709f0b6b28f9b4c9c3fefea9cc906dc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,5.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365986 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365987 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365986 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365986 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365986 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365987 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365986 1080 #uuid "fe775c71-99de-5cc3-aef5-17706e9eb83c" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365987 1081 "0" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365987 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365987 1084 #uuid "b70274dc-4d7b-579b-8df7-4cc9663948d1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365989 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365988 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365988 1077 "field-2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365988 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365989 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365988 1080 #uuid "4674f5a1-48aa-507e-b0b9-3defbef33eaf" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365989 1081 "5" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365989 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365989 1084 #uuid "56d593a5-e927-5feb-8c48-301f4a3f877e" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365991 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365993 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1069 "q-354994" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=5-\\frac{1}{2}t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1072 #uuid "77799534-8c26-5a8c-81b1-59219a3dda8a" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365990 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 5-\\frac{1}{2}t & =0 \\\\ t & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme values using the second derivative test. The second derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-\\frac{1}{2}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(10)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (5-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(4)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(4)-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{2}+C & =3 \\\\ 20-4+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity is given by $v(t)=5t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}-13.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(10)=5(10)-\\frac{1}{4}(10)^{2}-13=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365991 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365992 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365991 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365991 1077 "field-2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365991 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365992 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365991 1080 #uuid "991e8bfb-d216-5777-8b02-41d6beedd873" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365992 1081 "10" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365992 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365992 1084 #uuid "81fb9a2a-e974-5e21-a24c-b83847d0670c" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365993 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365994 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365993 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365993 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365993 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365994 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365993 1080 #uuid "3d560f3e-45c6-5297-9dbb-a7f86861cc41" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365994 1081 "12" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365994 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365994 1084 #uuid "65696180-4576-5e92-bc35-eb4097717b74" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365996 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365998 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1069 "q-355219" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=7-7{t}^{2},$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1072 #uuid "01deaa82-6ed4-5ec3-bb26-dc6ad125e782" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365995 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 7-7{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 7(1-t)(1+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t⩾0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-7{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14t \\\\ {v}^{″}(1) & =-14(1) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(1)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (7-7{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)-\\frac{7}{3}(2)^{3}+C & =1 \\\\ 14-\\frac{56}{3}+C & =1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{17}{3}+7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(1)=\\frac{17}{3}+7(1)-\\frac{7}{3}(1)^{3}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365996 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365997 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365996 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365996 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365996 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365997 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365996 1080 #uuid "5ac3bdfa-2313-5db4-aa3b-2fcdafe606ef" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365997 1081 "\\frac{31}{3}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365997 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365997 1084 #uuid "f80921a7-9ef8-51bc-ba03-c610b343031b" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365999 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365998 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365998 1077 "field-2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365998 1079 #edb/ref 17592186365999 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365998 1080 #uuid "9019baa6-75df-546b-b37a-ca7344b0b1e1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365999 1081 "1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365999 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186365999 1084 #uuid "e86f0a56-8589-5937-b33b-d876d2fcde71" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366001 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366000 1069 "q-355263" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366000 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{6}{{t}^{3}}$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=24\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366000 1072 #uuid "0fe72c46-3c48-5916-961d-5d6b9fd44782" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366000 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{{t}^{3}}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=24.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =-\\frac{3}{(1)^{2}}+C \\\\ 24 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+27.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+27 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{{t}^{2}} & =27 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solution $t=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366001 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366002 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366001 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366001 1077 "field-1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366001 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366002 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366001 1080 #uuid "1d2d504e-68af-56a6-9c47-add145f31