}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-3\\alpha +2(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t-4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha t+(-3\\alpha +2\\beta )=t-4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -3\\alpha +2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{5}{4},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{2t-5}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366192 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366193 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366194 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366195 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366196 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366194 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366191 1080 #uuid "b5c0fc63-5b76-5b1c-b861-02062bd63560" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366192 1081 "{y}_{p}(x)=8{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366192 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366192 1084 #uuid "a01fc108-f471-5f76-a5ee-417c7d24e5a8" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366193 1081 "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+7}{4}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366193 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366193 1084 #uuid "8a6d5d09-c35a-5526-9c7b-a7bd9eb06ae7" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366194 1081 "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5}{4}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366194 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366194 1084 #uuid "5eb1b9a9-8877-55ae-b463-b16761bb04b3" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366195 1081 "{y}_{p}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3" 131941395334