j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/358dcedefd029cdbd07f3c64b1264e568bfbac134b92a607e9d55c632c277b13.png)" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366407 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366408 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366409 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366410 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366411 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366411 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366406 1080 #uuid "e78ce83e-7416-5a1d-926b-9005c4697cd2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366407 1081 "images/e6/e6a72b4fb4a9c100996e7761407a2eb923d22f0d26832964bea2d4d6e3c90f2e.png" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366407 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366407 1084 #uuid "5e3b14f5-b75f-5e25-8b24-54bca66e3bc4" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366408 1081 "images/3a/3a7e4f6e534ec77a08e425b86d97f43535c376909c80aa6621d0061ce1127b45.png" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366408 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366408 1084 #uuid "41b12afb-9a58-5351-b7e6-d6d2dd1ece9c" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366409 1081 "images/ca/ca19bbcea3f01e99b0c606ad719686d84bca7b4dbfa1596f7b28e27fa494c44c.png" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366409 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366409 1084 #uuid "30cab3d3-ba95-50ea-81fe-afba08fde3d7" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366410 1081 "images/f8/f84c512bb1c78f49fe4f6711a1b4c7ca937befdb7993939e2eb94577f20ee057.png" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366410 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366410 1084 #uuid "2c66f658-d51d-5b20-aa3a-86a43c52e086" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366411 1081 "images/c6/c612d528e8fb432e4f63fdaae2649a4a41b0f3bed7751df5b5cc22074b57bb53.png" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366411 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366411 1084 #uuid "884d1d5f-d8a6-57da-a4a3-02e76a280253" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366413 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366412 1069 "q-81840" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366412 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6-2t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366412 1072 #uuid "19d63e1e-878a-5c14-84af-3c1645e3dcb8" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366412 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-2t) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6-2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1)-(1)^{2}+C & =2 \\\\ 6-1+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-{t}^{2}+6t-3.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=-(3)^{2}+6(3)-3=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366414 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366415 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366416 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366417 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366418 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366417 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366413 1080 #uuid "35fe62c1-6c0e-5da2-9b35-9e17db8f2d94" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366414 1081 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366414 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366414 1084 #uuid "2d890c58-af91-51f8-85ea-2bd07fad71ab" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366415 1081 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=9\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366415 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366415 1084 #uuid "75003568-9821-5daf-9547-5c8d08e948b2" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366416 1081 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366416 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366416 1084 #uuid "1fe9381d-5f8b-5423-8662-414e63ce1e57" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366417 1081 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366417 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366417 1084 #uuid "59ff1ecd-83ff-5776-a575-d84980260b87" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366418 1081 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=9\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366418 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366418 1084 #uuid "31b0f3bb-1a29-59a1-9bdc-cf6e4a5ef47f" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366420 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366419 1069 "q-82049" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366419 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time  in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366419 1072 #uuid "2bf4373d-fd90-5472-8b82-87ff2c920c93" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366419 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ 2t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-12\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\nTherefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =1 \\\\ 0+C & =1\\,⟹\\,C=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366421 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366422 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366423 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366424 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366425 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366425 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366420 1080 #uuid "ca8329e9-7ccd-5315-91fb-dae2ef3df42f" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366421 1081 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139533447 1