mation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0,x+z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366461 1072 #uuid "c81c0d34-9745-5e75-9229-e0ad0c633344" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366461 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366463 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366464 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366465 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366466 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366467 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366463 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366462 1080 #uuid "341f085e-62da-558a-b860-941516d5a7ea" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366463 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366463 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366463 1084 #uuid "3f82915f-c86b-5bde-b128-aa8feaf5eb7d" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366464 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366464 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366464 1084 #uuid "caee2230-e8a8-5838-bf16-144fe70f8dc0" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366465 1081 "III only" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366465 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366465 1084 #uuid "1fe410b3-519c-51ef-83b9-4ce4742d97aa" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366466 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366466 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366466 1084 #uuid "ebbe649a-1dd8-51db-8e67-99d497c82623" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366467 1081 "II only" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366467 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366467 1084 #uuid "d798f200-fdcc-516d-a281-81160b32cf81" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366469 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366468 1069 "q-84224" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366468 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3\\sqrt{t}-1,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366468 1072 #uuid "a56a54de-b33f-5937-8502-c313396447aa" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366468 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3\\sqrt{t}-1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3/2}-t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2(1)^{3/2}-1+C \\\\ 1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time wh