{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366491 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366492 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366494 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366495 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1077 "selection" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1079 #edb/ref 17592186366491 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366490 1080 #uuid "d824b74c-9e1f-5141-804b-27c8039bcb40" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366491 1081 "None of the statements are true" 131941