gned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-12{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-12{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-12] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4.$\nTherefore, the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{4}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is proportional to ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is the constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\alpha )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}) & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ (2\\alpha -12\\alpha ){x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ -10\\alpha {x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{x}^{4}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366575 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366576 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366577 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366578 