{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-10y=5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{5t}}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0+3\\alpha -10(\\alpha t+\\beta )=5t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-10\\alpha t-10\\beta +3\\alpha =t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -10\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{20},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366581 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366582 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366581 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366583 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [175921863665