}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+6\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+30{y}_{1}+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+30{y}_{1}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1})-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1}+25-25)-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1}+25)-75-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+5)^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-63 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+5)^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2} & =63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+5, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-7{z}_{2}^{2} & =63 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{21})^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes  $\\sqrt{21}$ and $3.$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366629 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366630 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366