da }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =8.$ Computing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366685 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366684 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366686 13194139533447 17592186045585 true] [17592186366684 1